er", "context": "Athenian democracy's legal and political structures framed the trial and execution of Pericles the Younger and his fellow generals in 406 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "pericles-the-younger", "sourceName": "Pericles the Younger", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "athens-greece", "targetName": "Athens, Greece", "context": "Pericles the Younger's life, military service, and trial were centered in Athens, Greece, the city he served."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Home City, Place of Trial"}, {"name": "Arginusae Islands, Greece", "role": "Site of Naval Victory"}, {"name": "Aegean Sea", "role": "Primary Naval Theater"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Pericles the Younger", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "pericles-the-younger"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1372571", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pericles_the_Younger"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Pericles the Younger's tragic fate at the Battle of Arginusae and subsequent trial illuminates the extreme political pressures and volatile nature of Athenian democracy during the Peloponnesian War, directly contributing to the depletion of experienced military leadership that hastened Athens' final defeat.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-03T23:02:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28685946833", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Pericles the Younger was an ancient Athenian strategos (general), the illegitimate son of famous Athenian leader Pericles by Aspasia.", "newValue": "Pericles the Younger, an Athenian strategos (general) executed in 405 BCE, was the illegitimate son of the renowned Athenian statesman Pericles and his brilliant Milesian consort, Aspasia. His brief but impactful military career unfolded during the final, tumultuous years of the Peloponnesian War, where he played a pivotal role in the Athenian navy's last major victory and, tragically, its subsequent catastrophic political fallout. His story highlights the brutal political realities and military desperation of late Classical Athens, particularly the severe penalties for perceived failure even among high-ranking commanders.

Appointed as one of the ten strategoi in 406 BCE, Pericles the Younger served alongside other prominent Athenians like Conon and Erasinides. His most notable engagement was the Battle of Arginusae in 406 BCE, a decisive naval victory for Athens against the Spartan fleet under Callicratidas, which temporarily broke the Spartan blockade of the Hellespont. Despite this crucial triumph, a severe storm prevented the Athenian commanders from rescuing shipwrecked sailors and recovering the bodies of the fallen. This failure led to a politically charged trial in Athens, where Pericles the Younger and five other generals were condemned to death under dubious legal pretexts.

The execution of Pericles the Younger and his co-commanders at Arginusae stands as a stark example of Athenian political instability and the harsh consequences of military defeat or perceived negligence, even in the face of victory. This event severely depleted Athens' experienced military leadership at a critical juncture, directly contributing to the city's final collapse in the Peloponnesian War just a year later at the Battle of Aegospotami. The trial and subsequent executions were later deemed illegal by the Athenians themselves, reflecting a period of profound self-reflection on their democratic processes and the dangers of mob rule."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-03T23:02:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28685946833", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "440 BCE, Athens"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "440 BCE, Athens"}], "newValue": ["The Peloponnesian War created a desperate need for experienced military leadership within the Athenian navy.", "Pericles the Elder's illustrious political career and relationship with Aspasia brought Pericles the Younger into the Athenian elite, despite his illegitimate birth.", "Athenian democracy, particularly during wartime, allowed for popular assemblies to exert significant and sometimes volatile influence over military command decisions and accountability."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-03T23:02:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28685946833", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "405 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "405 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The execution of Pericles the Younger and other stratego